  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "calculate_scalar",
    "file": "harfbuzz/src/hb-ot-var-gvar-table.hh",
    "description": "Heap OOB read in OT::TupleVarHeader::calculate_scalar(): it iterates i < coord_count and reads peak_tuple[i] (and possibly start/end tuple[i]) without verifying that the tuple arrays are actually that long in the current tuple record. For malformed gvar tuple headers, get_peak_tuple/get_start_tuple/get_end_tuple are derived from unchecked in-table data, so peak_tuple can be shorter/invalid while coord_count comes from font->num_coords (axisCount). This causes a 2-byte read past the end via F2DOT14/BEInt conversion. Crash manifests at BEInt<short,2>::operator short() in hb.hh, but the root cause is missing bounds validation in calculate_scalar before indexed tuple reads.",
    "conditions": "In hb-draw-fuzzer, a crafted font with a malformed gvar table reaches gvar::accelerator_t::apply_deltas_to_points(), which calls iterator.current_tuple->calculate_scalar(coords,num_coords,...). If tuple metadata indicates embedded/intermediate tuples but serialized tuple data is truncated/inconsistent, calculate_scalar indexes tuple coordinates up to coord_count and reads beyond available tuple bytes. The PoV triggers this during glyph draw path (hb_font_draw_glyph -> glyf get_path/get_points -> apply_deltas_to_points -> calculate_scalar)."
  },
